CAT = "nbdheCasePerio"; SEG = "CPA"
Q = [
("Case: 47-year-old man, smokes 20 cigarettes a day, no medications. Generalized 5 to 7 mm probing depths, little bleeding, interdental CAL up to 6 mm, no teeth lost to periodontitis. Why does his gingiva bleed so little?",
 "Nicotine constricts vessels", ["His disease is now inactive", "He brushes very well", "Calculus seals the pockets"],
 "Smoking constricts gingival vessels and dampens the inflammatory response, masking bleeding. Low bleeding in a smoker does not mean the disease is stable."),
("Case: 47-year-old man, smokes 20 cigarettes a day. Interdental CAL up to 6 mm at the worst site, no teeth lost to periodontitis, generalized pattern. Which stage and grade fit best?",
 "Stage III, grade C", ["Stage II, grade B", "Stage IV, grade A", "Stage I, grade C"],
 "CAL of 5 mm or more with no extra complexity is stage III. Smoking 10 or more cigarettes a day moves the grade to C."),
("Case: 52-year-old woman, healthy. Probing depths of 6 mm on molars with heavy subgingival calculus, bleeding on probing. Which instrument is best to finish root planing in a deep, narrow molar pocket?",
 "Mini-bladed Gracey curette", ["Large sickle scaler", "Universal curette, standard", "Hoe scaler"],
 "Mini-bladed Gracey curettes have a shorter, thinner blade and longer terminal shank that reach deep, narrow pockets and furcations."),
("Case: 52-year-old woman, healthy, generalized 4 to 6 mm pockets. Scaling and root planing is planned by quadrant. Which is the best reason to use local anesthesia?",
 "Comfort for deep root planing", ["To slow the bleeding at once", "To lower plaque scores", "To stop calculus forming"],
 "Root planing in deep pockets is uncomfortable. Anesthesia lets the hygienist work thoroughly; it does not change biofilm or calculus formation."),
("Case: 63-year-old man, at his 4-week reevaluation after scaling and root planing. Pockets fell from 6 mm to 4 mm, with no bleeding. What most explains the drop?",
 "Less edema, tighter tissue", ["New bone grew in the defects", "The gingiva grew coronally", "Calculus formed a new seal"],
 "After therapy the tissue tightens as edema resolves and a long junctional epithelium forms against the root. True bone regeneration is not expected from nonsurgical care."),
("Case: 63-year-old man, 4 weeks after scaling and root planing. Most sites are 3 to 4 mm without bleeding, but one site on tooth 3 remains 7 mm and bleeds. What is the best next step?",
 "Refer to a periodontist", ["Recall in 12 months and watch", "Polish and dismiss", "Place a sealant there"],
 "A deep site that still bleeds after therapy has not responded. Referral for surgical evaluation is appropriate; long recall would let it progress."),
("Case: 58-year-old woman with recession on tooth 22. The CEJ is visible, the gingival margin is 3 mm apical to it, and probing depth is 2 mm. What is the CAL?",
 "5 mm", ["1 mm", "2 mm", "3 mm"],
 "With recession, CAL = probing depth + recession = 2 + 3 = 5 mm."),
("Case: 44-year-old man, on nifedipine for hypertension. Firm, fibrotic gingival enlargement covers a third of the anterior crowns. Which drug class is the cause?",
 "Calcium channel blocker", ["Beta blocker", "ACE inhibitor", "Thiazide-type diuretic"],
 "Nifedipine is a calcium channel blocker linked to gingival overgrowth, as are phenytoin and cyclosporine. Good plaque control lessens it."),
("Case: 44-year-old man, nifedipine-related gingival enlargement. What should the hygienist tell him first?",
 "Plaque control limits overgrowth", ["Stop the drug on your own", "Surgery is never needed", "It means a gum cancer"],
 "Plaque control reduces the severity of drug-influenced enlargement. Only the physician may change the drug; surgery may still be needed if it persists."),
("Case: 71-year-old woman, takes an anticholinergic for overactive bladder. Root caries on several cervical surfaces, dry mucosa, uses a soft brush. Which home product is best?",
 "5,000 ppm fluoride toothpaste", ["Whitening toothpaste, daily", "Alcohol-based rinse", "Charcoal toothpaste"],
 "High-fluoride (1.1% NaF) toothpaste helps arrest and prevent root caries. Alcohol rinses dry the mouth further."),
("Case: 71-year-old woman with dry mouth from medications. She sips sweetened lemonade during the day. Which advice helps most?",
 "Sip plain water instead", ["Suck lemon drops", "Rinse with alcohol", "Drink soda for flow"],
 "Plain water keeps the mouth moist without acid or sugar. Sugary or acidic drinks and candies raise caries and erosion risk."),
("Case: 76-year-old man with arthritis in both hands. Plaque on most surfaces, struggles to grip his toothbrush. Which aid helps most?",
 "Power toothbrush", ["Unwaxed floss", "Toothpick holder", "Interdental tape"],
 "A power brush with a wide handle needs little dexterity. Floss is hard to use with arthritic hands; a floss holder or water flosser can help interdentally."),
("Case: 76-year-old man with a maxillary complete denture. The palate under the denture is red and smooth, the denture is worn day and night. What is the likely cause?",
 "Denture stomatitis", ["Squamous cell carcinoma", "Lichen planus", "Nicotine stomatitis"],
 "Denture stomatitis, linked to Candida and constant wear, gives redness under the denture base. Remove the denture nightly and clean it daily."),
("Case: 68-year-old woman with implants on teeth 19 and 30. Probing depths around the implants are 4 mm with bleeding, no bone loss on radiographs. Which diagnosis fits?",
 "Peri-implant mucositis", ["Peri-implantitis", "Failed osseointegration", "Healthy implant tissue"],
 "Bleeding without bone loss is peri-implant mucositis, which is reversible with better biofilm control and debridement."),
("Case: 68-year-old woman with implants. Which home aid is best for cleaning around the implant abutments?",
 "Interdental brush, coated wire", ["Toothpick with tape floss", "Metal pick", "Hard bristle brush"],
 "Interdental brushes with coated wire clean abutments without scratching titanium. Metal picks can damage the surface."),
("Case: 39-year-old woman. A finger on tooth 24 feels it vibrate when she taps her back teeth together. What is this sign called?",
 "Fremitus", ["Crepitus", "Ankylosis", "Abfraction"],
 "Fremitus is palpable vibration of a tooth in function, a sign of occlusal trauma. Crepitus is a grating sound in the TMJ; ankylosis is fusion of root to bone."),
("Case: 39-year-old woman, 5 mm pocket on the distal of tooth 2 with a furcation the Nabers probe passes through from buccal to distal. What is the furcation grade?",
 "Grade III", ["Grade I", "Grade II", "Grade IV"],
 "A probe passing completely through the furcation is grade III. Grade IV is through-and-through and visible clinically."),
("Case: 55-year-old man with a deep cervical enamel projection into the buccal furcation of tooth 30. Why does it matter?",
 "It favors furcation pockets", ["It prevents calculus there", "It strengthens the root", "It marks a root fracture"],
 "Cervical enamel projections block connective tissue attachment, so pockets and furcation involvement form there more easily."),
("Case: 45-year-old man, recently started doxycycline 20 mg twice daily after SRP. He asks why the dose is so low. What is the best answer?",
 "It slows tissue breakdown", ["It cures the gum infection", "It whitens the teeth", "It stops tartar forming"],
 "Subantimicrobial-dose doxycycline inhibits collagenase and does not act as an antibiotic. It is used alongside scaling and root planing."),
("Case: 45-year-old man after SRP has a 6 mm site on tooth 14. The dentist orders a local antimicrobial. When is it placed?",
 "After debridement of the site", ["Before any scaling", "Only at a one-year recall visit", "Instead of root planing"],
 "Locally delivered agents are adjuncts placed into the pocket after scaling and root planing, not a replacement for it."),
("Case: 50-year-old woman, chlorhexidine 0.12% prescribed after periodontal surgery. What side effect should she know about?",
 "Brown extrinsic stain", ["Gingival overgrowth", "Enamel erosion", "Black hairy tongue only"],
 "Chlorhexidine causes extrinsic brown staining and altered taste and can increase calculus. Stain is removed at the next visit."),
("Case: 50-year-old woman using chlorhexidine 0.12%. How long should she wait after brushing with toothpaste before rinsing?",
 "About 30 minutes", ["No wait needed", "At least 4 hours", "Overnight"],
 "Sodium lauryl sulfate in toothpaste can inactivate chlorhexidine, so a gap of about 30 minutes is often advised. She should not rinse or eat right after."),
("Case: 61-year-old man after a periodontal flap surgery 9 days ago, sutures present, mild tenderness. What is usually done today?",
 "Remove the sutures", ["Repeat the surgery", "Scale the surgical site", "Take a full mouth series"],
 "Sutures are usually removed 7 to 10 days after periodontal surgery, with gentle debridement and review of home care."),
("Case: 61-year-old man after periodontal surgery with root exposure. Teeth are sensitive to cold. Which in-office treatment occludes tubules?",
 "Fluoride varnish", ["Bleaching gel", "Phosphoric acid etch", "Sodium bicarbonate polish"],
 "Fluoride varnish occludes dentinal tubules and reduces sensitivity. Etching would open tubules further."),
("Case: 66-year-old woman with generalized 4 to 5 mm pockets and heavy tenacious calculus on lower anteriors. Which instrument is best for initial bulk removal there?",
 "Ultrasonic scaler", ["Gracey 13/14", "Explorer", "Periodontal probe"],
 "Ultrasonic scalers remove heavy calculus efficiently and flush the pocket. Curettes refine the surfaces after."),
("Case: 66-year-old woman. A right-handed hygienist now scales the mandibular anterior surfaces that face away from her. Where should she sit?",
 "11 to 12 o'clock", ["8 to 9 o'clock", "4 to 5 o'clock", "6 to 7 o'clock"],
 "For a right-handed clinician, anterior surfaces toward the operator are worked from about 8 to 9 o'clock and surfaces away from the operator from behind the patient, at about 11 to 12 o'clock."),
("Case: 49-year-old man, plaque-induced gingivitis, no attachment loss, bleeding on 35% of sites. What is the diagnosis under the 2017 classification?",
 "Generalized gingivitis", ["Localized plaque gingivitis", "Stage I periodontitis", "Periodontal health"],
 "Bleeding at 30% or more of sites is generalized gingivitis; 10 to 30% is localized. Without attachment loss, it is not periodontitis."),
("Case: 49-year-old man with gingivitis. Plaque Index 2.1, interdental plaque heavy, flosses rarely, wide embrasures. Which interdental aid fits best?",
 "Interdental brush", ["Waxed dental tape", "Rubber tip only", "Tongue scraper"],
 "Interdental brushes clean open embrasures and concave root surfaces better than floss, and are easier for many patients."),
("Case: 57-year-old woman with mesial root concavity on tooth 5 and a 5 mm pocket. Why is this site hard to clean?",
 "The concavity hides plaque", ["The root is too short", "The enamel is too thick", "The tooth is nonvital"],
 "The mesial concavity on a maxillary first premolar prevents floss from reaching the depression. An interdental brush cleans it better."),
("Case: 70-year-old man with a fixed bridge from teeth 28 to 30. Which aid lets him floss under the pontic?",
 "Floss threader", ["Toothpick", "End-tuft brush only", "Rubber tip stimulator"],
 "A floss threader or tufted floss passes under the pontic to clean the tissue side and the abutment proximal surfaces."),
("Case: 53-year-old man, history of periodontitis, well maintained. Probing depths stable at 3 to 4 mm, no bleeding. What recall interval is reasonable?",
 "3 to 4 months", ["Every 2 weeks", "Every 2 years", "Only when in pain"],
 "Treated periodontitis patients stay on periodontal maintenance at about 3 to 4 months, adjusted to stability."),
("Case: 42-year-old woman, ulcerated, painful papillae with a gray membrane and a foul odor after weeks of stress and little sleep. Which condition is likely?",
 "Necrotizing gingivitis", ["Desquamative gingivitis", "Primary herpes", "Pregnancy gingivitis"],
 "Stress, poor sleep, smoking and poor nutrition predispose to necrotizing gingivitis, with punched-out papillae and pain."),
("Case: 42-year-old woman with necrotizing gingivitis. What is the first treatment?",
 "Gentle debridement and rinse", ["Root planing under the flap", "Polishing with coarse pumice", "Immediate tooth extraction"],
 "Treatment starts with gentle debridement, oxidizing or chlorhexidine rinses, rest and nutrition, with antibiotics if there is systemic involvement."),
("Case: 59-year-old man with abfraction lesions on the cervical buccal of premolars and heavy wear facets. What is the likely cause?",
 "Occlusal stress", ["Sugary diet", "Acid reflux", "Overbrushing only"],
 "Abfraction is linked to flexural stress from occlusal loading, which concentrates at the cervical area. Toothbrush abrasion is a separate wear process."),
("Case: 59-year-old man who grinds his teeth at night with wear facets. What should the hygienist suggest?",
 "Ask about a night guard", ["Brush harder at night", "Stop flossing for now", "Use whitening strips"],
 "A dentist-fabricated occlusal guard protects the teeth from bruxism. The hygienist notes the wear and refers to the dentist."),
("Case: 64-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, HbA1c 8.4%, generalized periodontitis. Which statement about his care is correct?",
 "Periodontal care may help glucose", ["His glucose does not matter", "He cannot have scaling", "He needs antibiotics first"],
 "Treating periodontitis can modestly lower HbA1c, and poor control worsens periodontal disease. Schedule after a meal and check his glucose."),
]
